@page "/projects"

<PageTitle>Projects — Cole</PageTitle>

<section class="section">
    <h1>Projects</h1>
    <p class="section-intro">
        A few things I've built or worked on.
    </p>

    <div class="projects-grid">
        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>FLDCF Wizards</h3>
            <p>
                Built a data comparison tool that lets FLDCF employees easily manage applications for Medicaid, SNAP, and TANF — supporting millions of state assistance applications every year.
            </p>
            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">C#</span>
                <span class="tag">.NET</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure</span>
                <span class="tag">React</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>Datamart</h3>
            <p>
                I built a batch job that would grab data from an Oracle DB and then ingest it into Excel files daily. This was used as a source of truth while we were migrating to a new system, and it was used by multiple teams to validate their work against the old system.
            </p>

            <button class="read-more" @onclick="ToggleDataMartDetails" aria-expanded="@showFLDCFDetails">
                <span class="read-more-text">@((showFLDCFDetails) ? "Show less" : "Read more")</span>
                <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>

            @if (showDataMartDetails)
            {
                <div class="project-details">
                    <h4>Problem</h4>
                    <p>
                        FLDCF needed a direct, inspectable relationship to the data being pulled from their systems. As a way to verify the pipeline was accurate and investigate quickly if something looked off, rather than trusting a black-box process.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Approach</h4>
                    <p>
                        I built a daily C# batch job that ingests and aggregates 108M+ rows, then writes the output to Excel workbooks. The format was requested by FLDCF's team so they could open and cross-reference directly without needing separate tools. Since Excel has a hard cap on rows per workbook, the job splits the data across multiple workbooks automatically to stay under that limit while keeping the output usable.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Tradeoff</h4>
                    <p>
                        Excel isn't the most scalable output format for this volume of data, but it was what FLDCF wanted. They needed something their team could open and audit themselves, not just a database they'd have to query. Optimizing for their ability to independently verify the data mattered more than optimizing for elegance.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Outcome</h4>
                    <p>
                        A daily pipeline that ingests and aggregates 108M+ rows and writes complete SFTP output in under 8 minutes, giving FLDCF a transparent, self-service way to validate the data instead of relying on trust in the pipeline.
                    </p>
                </div>
            }

            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">C#</span>
                <span class="tag">.NET</span>
                <span class="tag">Batch</span>
                <span class="tag">MSSQL</span>
                <span class="tag">Oracle</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>FLDCF Worker Portal</h3>
            <p>
                Led the modernization of a legacy system into a new portal for FLDCF caseworkers to process assistance applications, migrating old code and databases to a modern framework and design. This became the foundation for the rest of a multi-year, multi-phase project.
            </p>

            <button class="read-more" @onclick="ToggleFLDCFDetails" aria-expanded="@showFLDCFDetails">
                <span class="read-more-text">@((showFLDCFDetails) ? "Show less" : "Read more")</span>
                <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>

            @if (showFLDCFDetails)
            {
                <div class="project-details">
                    <h4>Problem</h4>
                    <p>
                        FLDCF caseworkers were running Medicaid, SNAP, and TANF applications through a legacy system with outdated code and databases. Requests were slow, workarounds were common, and the system was a bottleneck for a modernization effort spanning multiple teams.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Approach</h4>
                    <p>
                        I led a 5-developer team rebuilding the portal on a modern React front end backed by C#/.NET APIs, migrating data out of the legacy databases as part of a multi-year, multi-phase program. We chose React and .NET to match the existing team's front-end skills and to align with the Azure ecosystem and backend tooling, which simplified integration with other services and existing infrastructure.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Tradeoff</h4>
                    <p>
                        We migrated incrementally, module-by-module, rather than performing a full cutover. This meant running both systems in parallel for a period — slower in the short term, but it ensured caseworkers never lost access mid-migration.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Outcome</h4>
                    <p>
                        Delivered UI features and integrations for a 200-person modernization program; built async API patterns keeping response times under 5 seconds despite heavy legacy data dependencies.
                    </p>
                </div>
            }

            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">C#</span>
                <span class="tag">.NET</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure</span>
                <span class="tag">React</span>
                <span class="tag">MSSQL</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>Monthly E&O Survey</h3>
            <p>
                Conceptualized and developed a monthly compliance survey sent to over 6,000 teammates to keep the company aligned with legal standards. Built while at Brown & Brown.
            </p>
            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">Azure Logic Apps</span>
                <span class="tag">C#</span>
                <span class="tag">.NET</span>
                <span class="tag">Power BI</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure Data Factory</span>
                <span class="tag">Survey Monkey</span>

            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>JUA</h3>
            <p>
                Modernization project at Brown & Brown: a secure file-sharing site where users upload and download large files, with granular security matrices applied down to the individual file and folder level.
            </p>
            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">Entra ID</span>
                <span class="tag">C#</span>
                <span class="tag">.NET</span>
                <span class="tag">Blazor</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure Data Factory</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure Blobs</span>
                <span class="tag">MSSQL</span>

            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
            <h3>Qualys Report</h3>
            <p>
                Built a daily pipeline that ingests Qualys vulnerability data into MSSQL and surfaces it in a Power BI report for management and the security team — helping them prioritize and track server fixes.
            </p>
            <button class="read-more" @onclick="ToggleQualysDetails" aria-expanded="@showFLDCFDetails">
                <span class="read-more-text">@((showFLDCFDetails) ? "Show less" : "Read more")</span>
                <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            @if (showQualysReportDetails)
            {
                <div class="project-details">
                    <h4>Problem</h4>
                    <p>
                        Security and management teams needed visibility into vulnerability data from Qualys, but the raw data wasn't in a form anyone could act on or prioritize against.
                    </p>

                    <h4>Approach</h4>
                    <p>
                        Built a daily ingestion pipeline into MSSQL using Azure Data Factory, then surfaced it through a Power BI report designed around what the security team actually needed to triage
                    </p>
                    <h4>Outcome</h4>
                    <p>
                        Gave management and the security team a daily, prioritized view of server vulnerabilities instead of raw scan output — turning a manual review process into something they could act on quickly.
                    </p>
                </div>
            }

            <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">Power BI</span>
                <span class="tag">Azure Data Factory</span>
                <span class="tag">MSSQL</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private bool showFLDCFDetails;
    private bool showDataMartDetails;
    private bool showQualysReportDetails;

    private void ToggleFLDCFDetails()
    {
        showFLDCFDetails = !showFLDCFDetails;
    }
    private void ToggleDataMartDetails()
    {
        showDataMartDetails = !showDataMartDetails;
    }
    private void ToggleQualysDetails()
    {
        showQualysReportDetails = !showQualysReportDetails;
    }
}